## Proof

Since $f$ is holomorphic with a zero of order $n \ge 1$ at $z=0$, we may write

$$f(z) = z^n\, g(z),$$

where $g$ is holomorphic in a neighborhood of $0$ and $g(0) \neq 0$.

Differentiating,

$$f'(z) = n\, z^{n-1} g(z) + z^n g'(z) = z^{n-1}\bigl(n\, g(z) + z\, g'(z)\bigr).$$

Form the quotient (valid for $z \neq 0$ near $0$, since $f'(z) \neq 0$ there because $n\,g(z) + z\,g'(z) \to n\,g(0) \neq 0$):

$$h(z) = \frac{f(z)}{f'(z)} = \frac{z^n g(z)}{z^{n-1}\bigl(n\, g(z) + z\, g'(z)\bigr)} = z \cdot \frac{g(z)}{n\, g(z) + z\, g'(z)}.$$

Define

$$G(z) := \frac{g(z)}{n\, g(z) + z\, g'(z)}.$$

The denominator $n\, g(z) + z\, g'(z)$ is holomorphic and equals $n\, g(0) \neq 0$ at $z=0$, so $G$ is holomorphic in a neighborhood of $0$. Thus $h(z) = z\, G(z)$ extends holomorphically across $0$ (the apparent singularity at $0$ is removable), and

$$h'(z) = G(z) + z\, G'(z).$$

Evaluating at $z = 0$:

$$h'(0) = G(0) + 0 = G(0) = \frac{g(0)}{n\, g(0) + 0} = \frac{g(0)}{n\, g(0)} = \frac{1}{n}.$$

Therefore,

$$\boxed{h'(0) = \frac{1}{n}}.$$

